// Type-checking for the test tree.
//
// `tsconfig.json` sets `include: ["src/**/*"]`, so the repo's own
// `typecheck` script has never looked at `tests/**` at all. That was
// invisible until a review noticed that "typecheck clean" was the only
// evidence backing a change set whose tests could not be executed —
// and the claim turned out to cover none of them.
//
// Running this config today reports 344 errors across 52 files. They
// are a mix of two things:
//
//   * genuine looseness in test code — implicit `any`, wrong argument
//     counts, over-broad casts on partial mocks;
//   * scope artifacts — tests that reach into `packages/db/src/...`
//     by relative path, which vitest resolves at runtime (those tests
//     pass) but which this config cannot see.
//
// This file exists so the number is measurable and can ratchet down,
// NOT as a gate: wiring it into CI today would simply be red. Fix
// errors opportunistically in files you are already touching, and drop
// the baseline in the script's comment when you do.
//
//   pnpm --filter @rovenue/api typecheck:tests
//
// `rootDir` is widened to the package root because `tsconfig.json`
// pins it to `src`, which would otherwise report every test file as
// "not under rootDir" and mask the real errors.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "rootDir": ".",
    "noEmit": true
  },
  // `scripts/**` and `openapi/**` ride along here for the same reason
  // `tests/**` does: `tsconfig.json` pins `include` to `src/**`, so neither
  // the OpenAPI generator nor the hand-written half of the document it reads
  // was type-checked by anything. A tool that documents the API is worth
  // exactly as much as its accuracy, and an unchecked generator can go stale
  // against the app it walks without a single red build.
  "include": ["src/**/*", "tests/**/*", "scripts/**/*", "openapi/**/*"]
}
